#: source/language/string-context.md:1
msgid "String context"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:3 source/language/string-context.md:19
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:5
msgid ""
"This is an advanced topic. The Nix language is designed to be used without "
"the programmer consciously dealing with string contexts or even knowing what "
"they are."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:8
msgid ""
"A string in the Nix language is not just a sequence of characters like "
"strings in other languages. It is actually a pair of a sequence of "
"characters and a _string context_. The string context is an (unordered) set "
"of _string context elements_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:12
msgid ""
"The purpose of string contexts is to collect non-string values attached to "
"strings via [string concatenation](./operators.md#string-concatenation), "
"[string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md), and similar operations. "
"The idea is that a user can reference other files when creating text files "
"through Nix expressions, without manually keeping track of the exact paths. "
"Nix will ensure that the all referenced files are accessible – that all "
"[store paths](../glossary.md#gloss-store-path) are [valid](../glossary."
"md#gloss-validity)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:21
msgid ""
"String contexts are _not_ explicitly manipulated in idiomatic Nix language "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:23
msgid "String context elements come in different forms:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:25
msgid ""
"[deriving path](../glossary.md#gloss-deriving-path){#string-context-element-"
"derived-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:27
msgid ""
"A string context element of this type is a [deriving path](../glossary."
"md#gloss-deriving-path). They can be either of type [constant](#string-"
"context-constant) or [output](#string-context-output), which correspond to "
"the types of deriving paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:30
msgid "\\[Constant string context elements\\]{#string-context-constant}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:32 source/language/string-context.md:54
#: source/language/string-context.md:83
msgid "**Example**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:34
msgid ""
"[`builtins.storePath`](./builtins.md#builtins-storePath) creates a string "
"with a single constant string context element:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:36
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/"
"ikwkxz4wwlp2g1428n7dy729cg1d9hin-hello-2.10\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:39 source/language/string-context.md:65
#: source/language/string-context.md:93
msgid "evaluates to"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:40
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/ikwkxz4wwlp2g1428n7dy729cg1d9hin-hello-2.10\" = {\n"
"    path = true;\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:52
msgid "\\[Output string context elements\\]{#string-context-output}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:56
msgid ""
"The behavior of string contexts are best demonstrated with a built-in "
"function that is still experimental: [`builtins.outputOf`](./builtins."
"md#builtins-outputOf). This example will _not_ work with stable Nix!"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:59
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext\n"
"  (builtins.outputOf\n"
"    (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-"
"hello-2.12.drv\")\n"
"    \"out\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:66
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-hello-2.12.drv\" = {\n"
"    outputs = [ \"out\" ];\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:76
msgid "\\[_derivation deep_\\]{#string-context-element-derivation-deep}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:78
msgid ""
"_derivation deep_ is an advanced feature intended to be used with the "
"[`exportReferencesGraph` derivation attribute](./advanced-attributes."
"html#adv-attr-exportReferencesGraph). A _derivation deep_ string context "
"element is a derivation path, and refers to both its outputs and the entire "
"build closure of that derivation: all its outputs, all the other derivations "
"the given derivation depends on, and all the outputs of those."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:85
msgid ""
"The best way to illustrate _derivation deep_ string contexts is with "
"[`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies). Take a regular constant string context element "
"pointing to a derivation, and transform it into a \"Derivation deep\" string "
"context element."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:88
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext\n"
"  (builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies\n"
"    (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-"
"hello-2.12.drv\"))\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:94
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-hello-2.12.drv\" = {\n"
"    allOutputs = true;\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:105
msgid "Inspecting string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:107
msgid ""
"Most basically, [`builtins.hasContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-hasContext) "
"will tell whether a string has a non-empty context."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:109
msgid ""
"When more granular information is needed, [`builtins.getContext`](./builtins."
"md#builtins-getContext) can be used. It creates an [attribute set](./types."
"md#type-attrs) representing the string context, which can be inspected as "
"usual."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:116
msgid "Clearing string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:118
msgid ""
"[`builtins.unsafeDiscardStringContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"unsafeDiscardStringContext) will make a copy of a string, but with an empty "
"string context. The returned string can be used in more ways, e.g. by "
"operators that require the string context to be empty. The requirement to "
"explicitly discard the string context in such use cases helps ensure that "
"string context elements are not lost by mistake. The \"unsafe\" marker is "
"only there to remind that Nix normally guarantees that dependencies are "
"tracked, whereas the returned string has lost them."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:123
msgid "Constructing string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:125
msgid ""
"[`builtins.appendContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-appendContext) will create "
"a copy of a string, but with additional string context elements. The context "
"is specified explicitly by an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) in the "
"format that [`builtins.hasContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-hasContext) "
"produces. A string with arbitrary contexts can be made like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:129
msgid ""
"Create a string with the desired string context elements. (The contents of "
"the string do not matter.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:131
msgid ""
"Dump its context with [`builtins.getContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"getContext)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:132
msgid ""
"Combine it with a base string and repeated [`builtins.appendContext`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-appendContext) calls."
msgstr ""
